WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:06.560
The very best horror movies give viewers scenes&nbsp;
that linger and fester in the mind like vengeful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:12.400
spirits, even after decades have passed. The&nbsp;
images are burned into our collective memories.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:16.800
Here are the scariest horror movie&nbsp;
scenes for every year since 1990.

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:22.640
In 1990, the psychological landscape of&nbsp;
the United States was complicated. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:27.680
paranoia and fragmentation of the titular&nbsp;
character in Jacob's Ladder would have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:32.800
somewhat familiar to mentally and emotionally&nbsp;
fatigued Americans at the end of the Cold War.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:38.880
But the singular horror of Jacob's Ladder extends&nbsp;
beyond an era-specific appeal. While exploring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:45.040
themes from PTSD to the biblical, the film employs&nbsp;
a unique brand of body horror through special&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:51.200
effects that were amazingly all done in camera,&nbsp;
with none digitally added in post-production.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:54.800
And that makes this hospital scene in&nbsp;
Jacob's Ladder all the more freaky,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:00.480
because as Jacob is bound to a gurney and wheeled&nbsp;
past people scrambling with supernatural fervor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:05.840
over the vents above him or rocking frantically&nbsp;
back and forth in straitjackets, through bloody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:11.200
curtains and past severed limbs, you know&nbsp;
all that horror was in some way very real.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:17.520
The scene in The Silence of the Lambs in which&nbsp;
serial killer Buffalo Bill stalks FBI agent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:22.240
Clarice Starling with night vision goggles&nbsp;
is a reminder of why the horror genre has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:27.600
long made use of first-person perspective,&nbsp;
most notably in found footage-style pieces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:32.560
to introduce a more panicked element that hits&nbsp;
much closer to home. The scene makes us feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:36.800
like we have to look over our shoulders&nbsp;
for a threat lurking just out of frame.

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:39.280
In this scene, the perspective offered to us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:43.520
is the first-person point of view of a&nbsp;
serial killer stalking our protagonist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:48.000
which makes us feel just as helpless and&nbsp;
at the mercy of his evil whims as Clarice.

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:53.680
Rather than pick up where the series left off,&nbsp;
Twin Peaks: Fire Walk With Me was set up as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.680 --> 00:02:00.000
prequel to David Lynch's iconic TV mystery Twin&nbsp;
Peaks, and didn't exactly tie up the series' loose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:06.560
ends. Possibly because he was able to show much&nbsp;
more of a dark side to society than on network TV,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:11.040
the most terrible things that happen in&nbsp;
Fire Walk With Me are the emotional traumas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:16.080
suffered by Laura Palmer. And we all know&nbsp;
this tragic story only leads to her death.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:20.160
While it may have been unconventional,&nbsp;
Lynch's direction in this prequel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:23.440
reached even deeper levels of&nbsp;
horror than it might have otherwise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:28.480
Which is why Laura Palmer's dream was&nbsp;
easily the scariest movie scene in 1992.

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:30.800
"Don't take the ring, Laura."

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:36.880
There's one Stephen King film adaptation that&nbsp;
literally has everything you could ever want,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:44.480
and it's 1993's Needful Things. Leland Gaunt, a&nbsp;
disguised demon, arrives in a small Maine town&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:48.000
and opens an antique shop that&nbsp;
offers items too good to be true.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.600
All he asks in return is that you&nbsp;
pull pranks on your neighbors.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:56.400
What's subtly disturbing about Needful Things&nbsp;
is that, although it's clear something is amiss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:02.160
Gaunt's meddling simply seems mischievous, even&nbsp;
harmless for a while, rather than outright evil.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:07.360
Arguably, it is the townspeople's own grudges,&nbsp;
prejudices, and pathologies that cause these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:11.760
conflicts to escalate, and ultimately,&nbsp;
multiple people to lose their lives.

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:15.280
That psychological underpinning&nbsp;
really sells the unconventional,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:18.880
chilling horror of two women fighting&nbsp;
to the death in a farmhouse kitchen.

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:23.600
Can the seventh installment of a&nbsp;
horror franchise ever really deliver?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:28.480
Wes Craven's New Nightmare managed to, with&nbsp;
one scene in particular. Part of what makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:34.080
Heather's nightmare in the hospital so scary is&nbsp;
simple. Hospitals are supposed to be safe spaces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:38.800
and she has finally fallen asleep. It's&nbsp;
also scary because it begins with Freddy's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:42.960
voice coming out of a child's mouth&nbsp;
as he stares coldly into her face.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:48.960
A doctor arrives, but Heather's hysteria increases&nbsp;
as she sees Freddy's hands protruding from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:54.320
doctor's coat, then looks up again to see that&nbsp;
Freddy has somehow infiltrated the hospital staff.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:58.160
The rapid escalation makes this&nbsp;
a genuinely scary sequence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:01.680
even more so because it appears&nbsp;
afterward to be all in her mind.

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:07.680
In 1995, we were blessed with the sixth&nbsp;
entry in the Halloween franchise. Halloween:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:11.680
The Curse of Michael Myers managed to&nbsp;
give us a pretty solid scare when Tommy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:14.720
played perfectly by Paul&nbsp;
Rudd, first meets Michael.

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:19.840
As Tommy struggles to free Kara from a locked&nbsp;
room, Michael appears at the other end of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:25.840
hallway. Tommy's response is one of bemusement&nbsp;
and disbelief, then fear, as he frantically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:30.960
tries to break the doorknob in time with an&nbsp;
unwieldy fire extinguisher, which makes all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:36.080
of his movements seem even more futile and frantic&nbsp;
and adds a little more scare value to the scene.

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:41.440
Scream's satirical approach to the horror&nbsp;
genre has helped it endure as a classic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:46.880
In fact, Scream's scariest moment lulled us&nbsp;
into a false sense of security and trust.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:51.840
Billy couldn't be the killer; he'd just been&nbsp;
attacked! The impact of the twist was heightened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:57.280
by the disconcerting sight of Billy nonchalantly&nbsp;
licking his own blood off his fingers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:01.920
revealing that it's just corn syrup, just&nbsp;
like they used for pig's blood in Carrie.

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:06.560
Couple that with Stu's sadistic grin,&nbsp;
the explanation that the two have had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:10.320
vendetta against Sid and her family and&nbsp;
murdered her mother, and the fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:13.920
Billy and Sid just slept together, and&nbsp;
it's enough to make your stomach turn.

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:18.560
David Lynch makes the list again with&nbsp;
Lost Highway and its "mystery man,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:23.360
who approaches protagonist Fred at a party&nbsp;
and claims to have met him at his own home.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:29.040
What follows is one of the most bizarre&nbsp;
sequences of 1997, made even creepier by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:35.440
the earnest but empty smile of the "mystery man,"&nbsp;
his lilting voice, and his peculiarly pale makeup.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:40.320
He insists he is at Fred's house at that moment,&nbsp;
even as he is standing right in front of him.

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:45.040
The fact that the "mystery man" is able&nbsp;
to appear simultaneously so amicable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:49.440
and threatening is unreal, and Robert&nbsp;
Blake's performance will haunt audiences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:52.560
as surely as the confusing&nbsp;
nightmare it foreshadows.

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:58.160
Even if you haven't seen this version of the&nbsp;
film, you'll recognize this scene from 1998's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:03.920
Ringu if you've seen the 2002 American remake.&nbsp;
The specter clambering out of the television&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:09.440
screen in this shot might be familiar to you as&nbsp;
Samara, but in the original movie her name was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:13.600
Sadako. And rather than seeing her&nbsp;
whole face as you do in The Ring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:18.640
in Ringu we only see her bulging eye,&nbsp;
which is arguably even more threatening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:23.360
and definitely helps lift this scene to the&nbsp;
number one slot for scary moments of 1998.

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:28.960
Before found footage became an overused&nbsp;
trope, The Blair Witch Project made it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:34.320
famous. The young filmmakers behind the project&nbsp;
did things in a completely new and effective way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:39.120
They ended up shooting about 20 hours of footage,&nbsp;
for a film with minimal scripted dialogue.

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:44.880
Editing trimmed their 20 hours of footage down to&nbsp;
the sub-90-minute run time you can watch today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:49.600
and one of the sequences that made the cut&nbsp;
ended up earning its place among the most iconic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:55.440
scenes in horror. Through a handheld camera, the&nbsp;
run-down house in the woods becomes a labyrinth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.440 --> 00:07:00.720
and by the time the scene concludes, you'll feel&nbsp;
you've aged years, not just an hour and a half.

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:02.800
"Where are you going?"

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:05.040
"I've got to return some videotapes."

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:10.560
We don't need to muse on the unlikeliness of&nbsp;
this scene, thanks to the comically absurd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:15.440
precedent set by American Psycho from&nbsp;
the opening scenes of the 2000 film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:20.640
Instead, we can just enjoy it in all its&nbsp;
gory glory. It starts when Patrick Bateman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:25.360
played by Christian Bale, kills Elizabeth&nbsp;
in some extremely bloody manner.

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:30.560
Christie, who had joined them for the encounter,&nbsp;
had climbed out of bed earlier and begins to run.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:36.400
On the way out, she sees the bodies of several&nbsp;
other women before finally escaping to the stairs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:42.720
which loop downward for many floors. Conveniently,&nbsp;
Bateman has a chainsaw, which he drops down the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:48.240
center of the stairwell, and it somehow lands on&nbsp;
and kills Christie in yet another bloody mess.

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:52.960
It's hard to raise a kid. It's even harder&nbsp;
when you look down at her and she has the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:58.160
wrinkled hand of an old woman, half-obscured&nbsp;
under a veil, who just keeps on insisting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:02.480
no matter how frantically you assert&nbsp;
otherwise, "I am your daughter."

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:06.960
This scene from 2001's The Others&nbsp;
scores extra creepy points because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:10.080
there's nothing really sinister&nbsp;
about the old woman under the veil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:14.640
except that she's, well, old. She&nbsp;
doesn't even have a creepy voice.

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:20.080
The real danger in this scene appears to be&nbsp;
Nicole Kidman's character, who violently shakes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:25.840
her daughter and tears at her veil to banish the&nbsp;
apparition, only to have it transform back into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:30.960
her confused, hurt daughter when witnesses arrive,&nbsp;
making the mother look like the real monster.

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:34.035
"You're wicked, wicked wicked!"

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:40.160
If there's one scene that teaches us never to&nbsp;
underestimate the simple elements of horror,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.160 --> 00:08:44.960
like the sudden appearance of a zombie, it's&nbsp;
the scene in the chapel from 28 Days Later.

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:49.680
Stumbling upon an abandoned church littered&nbsp;
with the dead bodies of the congregation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:54.080
is honestly pretty scary already, and you'd&nbsp;
think that a response to Jim's hopeful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:58.720
"Hello?" would have been just the relief he&nbsp;
needed. When that response comes, though, it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:04.080
from a zombie lurching towards him. He doesn't&nbsp;
immediately run. That changes quickly, though.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:10.800
Another zombie pops up, and soon, the whole horde&nbsp;
awakens. Jim's naivete, sticking around rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:15.360
than running at the first sight of the undead,&nbsp;
gave us one of our scariest zombie moments.

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:20.000
The scariest scene in the remake&nbsp;
of The Texas Chainsaw Massacre,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:24.480
and the year 2003, didn't&nbsp;
involve a single chainsaw.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:30.160
Instead, this foreboding scene is made extra&nbsp;
scary due to its pacing and sound design.

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:33.200
A group of pigs trundling&nbsp;
through the living room is weird,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:38.960
but not especially dangerous. A porcelain head&nbsp;
and sewing machine on a desk is definitely creepy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:44.240
Kemper sees an eerie old photograph before&nbsp;
hearing a sound from another room and continuing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.240 --> 00:09:50.240
his search for Erin. The backdrop of dismayed&nbsp;
black-and-white cartoon faces playing on the TV&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:55.040
as he is abruptly killed with a sledgehammer&nbsp;
is the perfect introduction to Leatherface.

00:09:55.760 --> 00:10:00.800
The sadistic game-master has taken various&nbsp;
forms in all manner of horror films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:05.680
but none of them quite gets under the skin&nbsp;
like the one in Saw who set the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:10.800
"reverse bear trap." This "game" is so&nbsp;
titled because the victim, Amanda, has a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:16.960
device strapped to her head that will, when the&nbsp;
timer goes off, rip her mouth open permanently.

00:10:16.960 --> 00:10:22.000
As if that weren't bad enough, Amanda has to&nbsp;
retrieve the only key to the device from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:28.080
stomach of her cell mate, using a knife. The&nbsp;
creepy green light and disorienting camera work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:33.920
add to the horror, but what really gets you is&nbsp;
the narration. Amanda is telling her own story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:37.920
so you know that she got out. But the&nbsp;
fact that you know how she had to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:41.200
it makes that prior knowledge&nbsp;
grim rather than comforting.

00:10:41.920 --> 00:10:48.240
The 2005 Rob Zombie flick The Devil's Rejects&nbsp;
surprises us when a hotel housekeeper finds one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:53.680
of the title trio's victims, Wendy, still&nbsp;
alive, hanging on the back of the door,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.680 --> 00:10:58.240
and wearing another victim's face.&nbsp;
She collapses onto the housekeeper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.240 --> 00:11:03.840
screaming wordlessly from behind the makeshift&nbsp;
mask before both of them make a run for it.

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:08.720
You can honestly see the next part coming.&nbsp;
Wendy can't see out of someone else's eye&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:13.120
holes and stumbles through the desert&nbsp;
and onto the road to flag down a car.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.120 --> 00:11:18.160
A truck runs her over. But the scariest&nbsp;
part wasn't her death. It was finding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.160 --> 00:11:22.560
her alive and watching her flail toward&nbsp;
her doom wearing another person's face.

00:11:23.360 --> 00:11:28.800
The fact that the 2006 remake of The Hills&nbsp;
Have Eyes centers around cannibal mutants in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:34.080
the Southwestern desert hints that there is a good&nbsp;
bit of sci-fi involved to give all of the horror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:38.560
an extra edge. The hills in which the&nbsp;
Carter family's car breaks down are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:44.080
plagued by mysterious disappearances connected&nbsp;
to nuclear testing in an old mining town nearby.

00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:48.640
"We absolutely had to see&nbsp;
the desert, didn't we Bob?"

00:11:48.640 --> 00:11:54.480
And if the cannibalism-ghost town combo isn't&nbsp;
enough, the impetus for the scariest scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:59.920
is the abduction of a baby, which leads to&nbsp;
Doug's ultimate confrontation with the mutants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.920 --> 00:12:03.440
which just might be the&nbsp;
scariest movie scene from 2006.

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:07.920
The first Paranormal Activity&nbsp;
film elevated the found footage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.920 --> 00:12:12.080
subgenre of horror cinema. Whereas&nbsp;
the cameras in The Blair Witch Project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.080 --> 00:12:16.640
were intentionally limited in scope,&nbsp;
completely subjective and without an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.640 --> 00:12:21.520
omniscient point of view, Paranormal&nbsp;
Activity gave us the opposite in 2007.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:27.520
The cameras offered a nearly full perspective of&nbsp;
each room they depicted, often unnervingly so.

00:12:27.520 --> 00:12:33.520
The setup in Paranormal Activity makes us squirm&nbsp;
so much because we can see "everything," but can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.520 --> 00:12:39.680
move, and it's night time, and we're all alone&nbsp;
with the characters. This cold distance heightens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.680 --> 00:12:46.160
the horror on the 21st and final night, easily&nbsp;
making it the scariest horror movie scene of 2007.

00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:52.320
This scene from The Strangers was shot so well&nbsp;
that its unnerving frame may be familiar to you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:57.280
from the movie's promotional poster, for which&nbsp;
it was eventually used. Though the shot itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.280 --> 00:13:02.800
is about as static as a still image for a few&nbsp;
moments, it's the chilling stillness combined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.800 --> 00:13:08.240
with Liv Tyler's slow, casual movements&nbsp;
that make it so creepy in the film itself.

00:13:08.240 --> 00:13:12.000
It's also creepy just how long we&nbsp;
have to sit and watch and wait,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:16.480
knowing that the intruder is standing mere&nbsp;
feet away and the character doesn't know it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.480 --> 00:13:21.360
When will she realize that someone is lurking&nbsp;
and how will it happen? Some jump scares are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.360 --> 00:13:26.480
frightening because they come out of nowhere, but&nbsp;
this one reveals a piece of the scare ahead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.480 --> 00:13:31.520
time, leaving us anxiously expecting another one&nbsp;
at any moment because we know it must be coming.

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:36.320
Lars Von Trier is no stranger&nbsp;
to being condemned for his art.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.320 --> 00:13:41.120
Many of his films have earned bans&nbsp;
and walkouts. His 2009 experimental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.120 --> 00:13:46.480
horror film Antichrist was even banned in&nbsp;
France seven years after it was released.

00:13:46.480 --> 00:13:50.320
This film features many of the&nbsp;
most disturbing scenes imaginable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.320 --> 00:13:54.480
but one that really sticks is the fox&nbsp;
disemboweling itself in the woods.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:58.880
When Willem Dafoe's character stumbles&nbsp;
upon the fox, he finds the animal ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.880 --> 00:14:04.320
so slowly pulling the flesh away from its&nbsp;
body until a lean strip of it just snaps off.

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:06.720
While the fox's subsequent announcement that...

00:14:06.720 --> 00:14:11.520
"Chaos reigns."

00:14:11.520 --> 00:14:15.200
...is viewed by some fans as&nbsp;
so ridiculous as to provide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.200 --> 00:14:19.680
unintentional comic relief, the moments&nbsp;
preceding it will chill you to the bone.

00:14:20.480 --> 00:14:26.000
What makes this Insidious scene, in which the&nbsp;
red demon face briefly appears behind Josh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:30.800
played by Patrick Wilson, work so well is the&nbsp;
fact that it hardly does anything different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.800 --> 00:14:35.680
from the traditional jump scare beyond a&nbsp;
few key elements. We've seen faces appear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.680 --> 00:14:41.440
behind people's shoulders before, and we've seen&nbsp;
people freak out at something only they can see.

00:14:41.440 --> 00:14:43.360
But what's compelling about this scene is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.360 --> 00:14:47.520
the characters are talking about the&nbsp;
demonic presence at that very moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.520 --> 00:14:54.480
and then it appears, brazenly, as if daring them&nbsp;
to speak more. The red color is also a vibrant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.480 --> 00:14:59.600
attention-grabbing contrast to the shadowy&nbsp;
forms demons typically take in horror films.

00:14:59.600 --> 00:15:02.560
Most importantly, the demon&nbsp;
appears in broad daylight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.560 --> 00:15:07.440
while characters are seated in a sunny room&nbsp;
around a table. This demon was just stopping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.440 --> 00:15:11.840
by because it could, and now the family&nbsp;
has to find a way to go on with their day.

00:15:12.560 --> 00:15:17.280
Most horror movies build tension in a manner&nbsp;
that revolves around the source of horror itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.280 --> 00:15:21.120
like a poltergeist or serial&nbsp;
killer. But in 2011's You're Next,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.680 --> 00:15:27.280
the source of tension leading up to its scariest&nbsp;
moments is almost always the characters.

00:15:27.280 --> 00:15:32.560
The family's bickering dominates just about&nbsp;
every scene, with the camera and the audience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.560 --> 00:15:37.920
scrambling to keep up. This creates a sense&nbsp;
of unease, not unlike scenes in which someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.920 --> 00:15:42.560
is being chased or hears strange noises and&nbsp;
keeps turning to look over their shoulder.

00:15:43.200 --> 00:15:46.240
"Excuse me? You say that like it's my fault."

00:15:47.360 --> 00:15:52.480
As the details and drama unfold, we learn that&nbsp;
this family is so dysfunctional that a couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.480 --> 00:15:56.920
of the kids have hired hitmen in creepy,&nbsp;
cultish animal masks to disrupt dinnertime.

00:15:58.080 --> 00:16:03.600
Genuinely frightening from start to finish,&nbsp;
Sinister makes it hard to pick the scariest scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.600 --> 00:16:09.600
but you know that this film is where you'll find&nbsp;
2012's winner. As many delightfully disturbing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.600 --> 00:16:16.080
moments as there are, protagonist Ellison, played&nbsp;
by Ethan Hawke, stumbles upon the best one early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.080 --> 00:16:21.040
in the film when he's digging through old home&nbsp;
movies in the attic of his family's new house.

00:16:21.040 --> 00:16:26.720
These movies depict the murders of the previous&nbsp;
family, filmed on a handheld camera. For one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.720 --> 00:16:31.520
thing, even if you can reasonably guess, you&nbsp;
really don't know what's coming. You're not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.520 --> 00:16:36.880
sure why the family made a home video of their&nbsp;
lawnmower at night, until you encounter the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.880 --> 00:16:42.240
artful and unconventional jump scare of the&nbsp;
year. It's not a demon jumping out at you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.240 --> 00:16:47.360
but it's a window into the fact that something&nbsp;
absolutely sinister happened at this house.

00:16:48.160 --> 00:16:53.360
If "things that go bump in the night" are standard&nbsp;
horror fare, The Conjuring was a bit of a horror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.360 --> 00:16:59.920
renaissance. Early on, the film equips itself&nbsp;
for sound-based scares. The Perron family has a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.920 --> 00:17:05.920
special game they like to play called "hide and&nbsp;
clap," which is like a variation on Marco Polo.

00:17:05.920 --> 00:17:07.280
"Yeah, let's play!"

00:17:07.280 --> 00:17:12.320
In this scene, the creepy sound apparently&nbsp;
comes from Cindy sleepwalking and banging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.320 --> 00:17:16.880
her head against the door. But after&nbsp;
she returns to bed, the sound starts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.880 --> 00:17:21.600
again. While a minor misdirection, it&nbsp;
was effective. We thought we had ruled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.600 --> 00:17:25.840
out the supernatural as the source of the sound&nbsp;
when we discovered that Cindy was causing it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.840 --> 00:17:30.320
meaning that we might have jumped when it resumed,&nbsp;
or at the very least went on higher alert,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.320 --> 00:17:34.960
inadvertently priming ourselves for the fright&nbsp;
of seeing the witch on top of the wardrobe.

00:17:35.520 --> 00:17:40.560
To make fear more tangible to its audience,&nbsp;
one approach is to make it more intimate. Many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:45.760
directors choose to accomplish this through&nbsp;
cinematography or editing, but It Follows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.760 --> 00:17:51.520
takes a decidedly unique and modern approach&nbsp;
by tying its horror to sexual intercourse.

00:17:52.080 --> 00:17:57.440
For much of the film, we don't see the demonic&nbsp;
entity that passes from person to person after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.440 --> 00:18:02.960
they've done the deed, and when we do, it takes&nbsp;
a different form for each victim. First, it is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.960 --> 00:18:09.280
woman, which only Jay, the afflicted young woman,&nbsp;
can see. However, the specter can take any form&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.280 --> 00:18:14.800
it pleases and could be anyone, which would be&nbsp;
terrifying on its own, but its next iteration, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.800 --> 00:18:19.840
Tall Man who causes Jay to leap out the window,&nbsp;
is the stuff of your nightmares' nightmares.

00:18:20.560 --> 00:18:25.120
You know you've seen a great horror movie&nbsp;
when a glorified folk tale can reach nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.120 --> 00:18:29.360
400 years through the centuries and&nbsp;
still send chills down your spine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:29.920 --> 00:18:34.960
especially when the title menace is only&nbsp;
glimpsed fleetingly for most of the story.

00:18:34.960 --> 00:18:40.080
In The Witch's scariest scene, the witches&nbsp;
have barely revealed themselves thus far.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.080 --> 00:18:44.880
That doesn't stop the family from going on a&nbsp;
witch hunt, with only each other as targets.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:44.880 --> 00:18:49.200
Ultimately, with a little supernatural&nbsp;
meddling, they tear each other apart.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:49.200 --> 00:18:53.600
It's certainly creepy when the witch appears&nbsp;
in the shed without entering through the door,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:53.600 --> 00:18:57.520
but it's even more horrifying that the&nbsp;
children's parents lock them out there.

00:18:57.520 --> 00:19:02.400
Interspersed with and exacerbating this&nbsp;
tragic terror are shots of Thomasin's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:02.400 --> 00:19:07.760
mother nursing what she sees as her baby,&nbsp;
while in reality, a crow gnaws at her chest.

00:19:08.640 --> 00:19:14.800
Sibling rivalry can get bitter, but you'd never&nbsp;
expect it to literally taste sweet. Apparently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:14.800 --> 00:19:21.200
though, if you come from a family of cannibals,&nbsp;
it can. Raw's Justine, formerly a lifelong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.200 --> 00:19:26.320
vegetarian, doesn't think about or behave&nbsp;
around human flesh as if it were just another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:26.320 --> 00:19:32.720
food or dietary preference. Her cravings are&nbsp;
pathological, primal, and uncontrollable.

00:19:32.720 --> 00:19:38.000
It's genuinely terrifying to watch her go through&nbsp;
her transformation and discover that her body&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:44.960
can only survive on human meat. But watching her&nbsp;
examine her sister's accidentally severed finger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:44.960 --> 00:19:50.000
then nibble it, then pick it clean, while&nbsp;
the soundtrack builds and her sister lies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.000 --> 00:19:55.040
unconscious in the next room, is one of the&nbsp;
most alarming things you'll see this year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:55.040 --> 00:20:00.080
if you didn't see Raw when it premiered in&nbsp;
2016. It's certainly the most alarming thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:00.080 --> 00:20:04.800
Justine's sister saw when she woke up just&nbsp;
in time to see Justine finishing her snack.

00:20:05.680 --> 00:20:11.760
One of the most horrifying thoughts is that true&nbsp;
horror exists not in a private, hypnosis-induced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:11.760 --> 00:20:17.280
nightmare but rather endemically in our way of&nbsp;
life. Filmmaker Jordan Peele explained that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:17.280 --> 00:20:22.160
Sunken Place in Get Out was a symbol for the&nbsp;
marginalized and all the systems that keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:22.160 --> 00:20:27.840
marginalized communities oppressed, specifically&nbsp;
in this case the impact of the prison-industrial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:27.840 --> 00:20:32.640
complex on Black communities as well&nbsp;
as the lack of representation in film.

00:20:32.640 --> 00:20:38.000
It's frightening to see Chris from the outside,&nbsp;
paralyzed but present, versus the inside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:38.000 --> 00:20:43.680
sunken and silenced. The Sunken Place makes&nbsp;
every other second of Get Out that much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:43.680 --> 00:20:49.360
terrifying by offering the context and revealing&nbsp;
the nature of Logan's outburst and other clues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:49.360 --> 00:20:55.360
earlier in the film. What may seem inconsequential&nbsp;
or "not quite right" in film and life to those who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:55.360 --> 00:21:01.840
only see a thirty-second scene is flat-out wrong&nbsp;
for those whose vantage point is the Sunken Place.

00:21:01.920 --> 00:21:06.080
If you're ever struggling to decide&nbsp;
between a horror movie and a dance movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:06.080 --> 00:21:10.160
you need look no further than&nbsp;
the 2018 version of Suspiria,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:10.160 --> 00:21:14.880
and after one early scene, you might&nbsp;
not want to look any further, period.

00:21:14.880 --> 00:21:22.160
Not exactly a remake of the color-saturated&nbsp;
1977 classic, the 2018 version follows similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:22.160 --> 00:21:27.920
themes and characters but expands greatly on the&nbsp;
more abstract realm of emotional provocation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:27.920 --> 00:21:33.920
At least, that was director Luca Guadagnino's&nbsp;
intent, and provocative doesn't even begin to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:33.920 --> 00:21:38.480
cover the scene of poor Olga's death at&nbsp;
the unknowing hands and feet of Suzy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:38.480 --> 00:21:45.360
whose dance has been somehow supernaturally&nbsp;
linked to Olga's helpless body. As Suzy dances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:45.360 --> 00:21:50.800
Olga's body is tossed and contorted&nbsp;
until she is deformed beyond recognition.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:50.800 --> 00:21:55.280
While the original movie doesn't expose&nbsp;
the undercurrents of witchcraft right away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:55.280 --> 00:22:00.880
2018's wastes no time in doing so,&nbsp;
in overwhelmingly gruesome fashion.

00:22:01.600 --> 00:22:06.880
The scariest moment in Midsommar actually&nbsp;
plays out on the western side of the Atlantic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:06.880 --> 00:22:10.960
not with free falls and sledgehammers&nbsp;
in the idyllic Swedish mountains.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:10.960 --> 00:22:12.960
Not that the rest of the film doesn't deliver,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:12.960 --> 00:22:18.480
but Dani's family's deaths at the hands of her&nbsp;
sister is more disturbing than anything else.

00:22:18.480 --> 00:22:23.600
The sound design of the reveal is piercing,&nbsp;
blending perfectly with Dani's screams. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:23.600 --> 00:22:28.720
cinematography and editing are dreamlike, with&nbsp;
first responders moving in slow motion through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:28.720 --> 00:22:33.680
darkness of the family's home. There's something&nbsp;
extra sinister about killing your parents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:33.680 --> 00:22:38.240
in their sleep, not to mention the elaborate&nbsp;
lengths to which the troubled girl goes.

00:22:38.800 --> 00:22:43.440
Plus, there's the disquieting realization that&nbsp;
when Dani called her parents earlier in the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:43.440 --> 00:22:48.160
to say she thought something was wrong, and the&nbsp;
phone rang next to them as they appeared to sleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:48.160 --> 00:22:49.920
they were probably already dead.

00:22:50.560 --> 00:22:53.680
What do you think of when you think&nbsp;
of "ghosting" someone? If you define&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:53.680 --> 00:22:58.720
it the way most millennials do, as ending&nbsp;
a relationship with someone by cutting off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:58.720 --> 00:23:03.760
all contact without warning or explanation,&nbsp;
then Adrian's behavior in The Invisible Man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:03.760 --> 00:23:09.280
of remaining in someone's life whether they want&nbsp;
you there or not, is pretty much the opposite.

00:23:09.280 --> 00:23:11.280
But you could say that Adrian "ghosts"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:11.280 --> 00:23:16.720
Cecilia because that is what he literally&nbsp;
becomes. He vanishes from view, yet tortures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:16.720 --> 00:23:22.240
her in plain sight while remaining completely&nbsp;
unseen and making her concerns seem crazy.

00:23:22.240 --> 00:23:26.320
His actions eventually escalate&nbsp;
beyond psychological abuse and into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:26.320 --> 00:23:29.760
taking Cecilia's sister Emily's life in public,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:29.760 --> 00:23:34.880
right in front of her, and framing her for it. An&nbsp;
even more chilling element of the public murder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:34.880 --> 00:23:40.720
is Adrian's remorseless display of dominance&nbsp;
and manipulation in a completely inhuman act.

